1
00:00:02,060 --> 00:00:03,290
一个想法
It is an idea

2
00:00:03,400 --> 00:00:08,020
催生了仇恨、战争和杀戮
that has spawned hatred, war, and genocide.

3
00:00:08,140 --> 00:00:11,800
这是我们能提出的
It is one of the most polarizing questions

4
00:00:11,910 --> 00:00:14,500
最极端的问题
we could ever ask.

5
00:00:14,610 --> 00:00:18,100
不同的种族是否只是看起来不一样？
Do different races not just look different?

6
00:00:18,210 --> 00:00:21,370
还是他们根本上就是不同的？
Are they fundamentally different?

7
00:00:21,480 --> 00:00:26,440
当将来的高等人类回首往事
Will a future race of advanced humans look back

8
00:00:26,560 --> 00:00:30,360
他们看来，我们是否天资低劣？
and see all of us as a vastly inferior breed?

9
00:00:31,660 --> 00:00:34,960
有没有优越种族？
Could there be a superior race?

10
00:00:40,940 --> 00:00:46,270
《同摩根·弗里曼一起穿越虫洞》
空间，时间，生命本身
Space, time, life itself.

11
00:00:48,340 --> 00:00:52,740
宇宙的秘密，尽在穿越虫洞
The secrets of the cosmos lie through the wormhole.

12
00:00:57,340 --> 00:01:02,240
是否存在优等种族？

13
00:01:05,830 --> 00:01:08,180
提到种族差异
Speak of racial differences,

14
00:01:08,300 --> 00:01:11,660
一定会点燃你的激情
and you're bound to inflame passions.

15
00:01:11,770 --> 00:01:13,560
波斯尼亚和塞尔维亚
Bosnian and Serb.

16
00:01:13,670 --> 00:01:16,130
日本人和韩国人
Japanese and Korean.

17
00:01:16,240 --> 00:01:17,960
黑人和白人
Black and White.

18
00:01:18,070 --> 00:01:20,300
关于某一种族
The belief that one group's germ line

19
00:01:20,410 --> 00:01:21,770
比其他种族优越的信念
is superior to another's

20
00:01:21,880 --> 00:01:24,530
数千年来一直纠缠着历史
has haunted history for thousands of years.

21
00:01:24,650 --> 00:01:29,140
但是现在已经是DNA技术的时代
But now we're in the age of DNA technology.

22
00:01:29,250 --> 00:01:33,540
我们可以从这些化学细链中
We can track down minute differences

23
00:01:33,660 --> 00:01:35,610
追寻细微的差异
in these chemical strands

24
00:01:35,730 --> 00:01:38,720
也许可能发现是什么使非洲人和印第安人
and perhaps discover whether they make Africans

25
00:01:38,830 --> 00:01:41,520
不光是看起来不同
not just look different from Indians,

26
00:01:41,630 --> 00:01:44,490
而且想法也不一样
but also think differently.

27
00:01:44,600 --> 00:01:49,830
长期以来，科学一直被滥用以试图支持偏见
Science has long been misused to try and prop up bigotry,

28
00:01:49,940 --> 00:01:52,800
但敢于提出“我们有什么不同？”这个问题
but daring to ask how we might be different

29
00:01:52,910 --> 00:01:54,600
是重要的
is important.

30
00:01:54,710 --> 00:01:56,900
因为答案可以告诉我们
Because the answers could tell us

31
00:01:57,010 --> 00:02:00,710
人类整个物种将去向何方
where the entire human species is headed.

32
00:02:04,490 --> 00:02:08,620
我在密西西比长大，邻居都是黑人
I grew up in an all-black neighborhood in Mississippi.

33
00:02:10,330 --> 00:02:15,550
我从未想过 我是否比别人更好或更差劲
I never thought of myself as better or worse than anyone.

34
00:02:15,670 --> 00:02:17,090
但是小镇四周
But all around town,

35
00:02:17,200 --> 00:02:21,570
有迹象（牌子）表明，别人想过这个问题
there were signs that other people did.

36
00:02:24,810 --> 00:02:29,430
马丁·路德·金 有种族平等的梦想
Dr. Martin Luther King Jr. had a dream of racial equality.

37
00:02:29,550 --> 00:02:31,000
在过去的50年间
And in the last 50 years,

38
00:02:31,110 --> 00:02:33,300
我们朝着
we've taken some steps

39
00:02:33,420 --> 00:02:36,310
不以肤色而论的世界迈出了脚步
toward the colorblind world he imagined.

40
00:02:36,420 --> 00:02:40,740
而科学家依然试图明确 种族 究竟意味着什么
But scientists are still trying to understand what race means,

41
00:02:40,860 --> 00:02:45,120
或者它根本没有任何科学意义
or if it has any scientific meaning at all.

42
00:02:45,230 --> 00:02:48,750
种族只是外在肤色问题？
Is race only skin deep?

43
00:02:48,870 --> 00:02:52,460
还是内部的、看不到的什么东西
Or is there something internal, invisible,

44
00:02:52,570 --> 00:02:55,760
产生了种族的差异？
that sets the races apart?

45
00:02:55,870 --> 00:02:59,560
进化生物学家 安迪·布劳尔
Evolutionary biologist Andy Brower

46
00:02:59,680 --> 00:03:02,870
认为我们能弄懂“种族”的含义
thinks we can understand what the word "race" means

47
00:03:02,980 --> 00:03:05,300
通过观察其他物种
by looking at another species,

48
00:03:05,410 --> 00:03:08,670
他觉得，他为研究这一物种而生
one he feels he was born to study.

49
00:03:08,780 --> 00:03:12,510
我的父母都是蝴蝶研究领域的博士
Both my parents worked on butterflies for their PhDs

50
00:03:12,620 --> 00:03:14,290
早在50年代
back in the '50s.

51
00:03:15,740 --> 00:03:19,690
我从五岁起就对这着迷
So I just became fascinated by that at 5 years old,

52
00:03:19,800 --> 00:03:22,990
我小的时候收集蝴蝶
and I've had a little butterfly collection as a kid.

53
00:03:23,440 --> 00:03:26,040
从一开始，它就在我的基因中
Definitely in my DNA, right from the start.

54
00:03:26,730 --> 00:03:29,550
安迪研究釉蛱蝶（一种色彩缤纷的蝴蝶）
Andy studies Heliconius butterflies.

55
00:03:30,320 --> 00:03:31,980
他追随着整个西半球上
He has followed their fluttering wings

56
00:03:32,090 --> 00:03:34,540
它们颤动的翅膀
all over the Western Hemisphere.

57
00:03:34,870 --> 00:03:38,580
他着迷于它们翅膀上精致的图案
As much as he is enchanted by their elaborate wing markings,

58
00:03:38,690 --> 00:03:42,620
他知道美丽只在外表
he knows that their beauty is only superficial.

59
00:03:43,010 --> 00:03:44,540
它们是有毒的蝴蝶
They're poisonous butterflies,

60
00:03:44,650 --> 00:03:46,940
他们有着类似的翅膀图案
and they have mimetic wing patterns

61
00:03:47,050 --> 00:03:50,570
这是对潜在的捕食者，通常是鸟类的宣告
so that they are advertising the fact that they taste bad

62
00:03:50,680 --> 00:03:53,840
它们口感糟糕
to potential predators, birds, usually.

63
00:03:53,950 --> 00:03:57,080
所以越多的蝴蝶现出共同的颜色模式
So that the more butterflies exhibit a common color pattern,

64
00:03:57,190 --> 00:03:59,810
鸟类越能容易地辨认出它们
the easier it is for the birds to recognize that,

65
00:03:59,930 --> 00:04:02,120
“好吧，那边那个红黄相间的东西
"Okay, there's one of those red and yellow things.

66
00:04:02,230 --> 00:04:04,080
它们吃起来真糟糕，我还是不要惹它”
They taste bad. I'm leaving those alone."

67
00:04:04,200 --> 00:04:06,290
但是不同地区的釉蛱蝶
But Heliconius butterflies

68
00:04:06,400 --> 00:04:08,920
看起来并不一样
do not look the same everywhere.

69
00:04:09,040 --> 00:04:10,890
南美某地的一群蛱蝶
A group in one region of South America

70
00:04:11,000 --> 00:04:12,860
有着完全不同的翅膀图案
has completely different wing patterns

71
00:04:12,970 --> 00:04:16,360
同河对岸的另一群
from another group that lives just across a river

72
00:04:16,480 --> 00:04:20,300
或者山那边的一群相比
or another on the other side of a mountain.

73
00:04:20,410 --> 00:04:23,970
这里有一盒蝴蝶，它们同属一个物种
This is a box of butterflies that are all the same species.

74
00:04:24,080 --> 00:04:27,110
在法属圭亚那（南美北部），前翅上有一条红杠
In French Guiana, a red band is on the fore wing

75
00:04:27,220 --> 00:04:29,610
后翅基本上是黑的
and the hind wing is basically black.

76
00:04:29,720 --> 00:04:30,840
在更远的南方
And then, further south,

77
00:04:30,960 --> 00:04:34,310
有红色射线，和黄色区域
you have red rays and the yellow areas.

78
00:04:34,430 --> 00:04:36,250
中间的这两行
These two rows in the middle here,

79
00:04:36,360 --> 00:04:37,620
颜色不同
the colors are different.

80
00:04:37,730 --> 00:04:39,320
翅膀图案之所以不同
The wing pattern differences

81
00:04:39,430 --> 00:04:41,460
可能是对当地鸟类
are probably driven by which colors and patterns

82
00:04:41,570 --> 00:04:45,030
某种图案看起来更清晰
stand out most clearly to the local bird population.

83
00:04:45,330 --> 00:04:46,890
这些看起来不同的品种
But these different-looking varieties --

84
00:04:47,820 --> 00:04:49,470
可以被称为种族
you could call them races.

85
00:04:49,880 --> 00:04:53,540
似乎拥有完全相同的内在生物学特征
Appear to have identical inner biologies.

86
00:04:53,910 --> 00:04:55,430
他们气味一致
They smell the same,

87
00:04:55,540 --> 00:04:57,860
它们相认，有成为配偶的潜质
they recognize each other as being potential mates.

88
00:04:57,980 --> 00:05:00,410
这样，它们产生混血的后代
And so they produce hybrid offspring.

89
00:05:00,810 --> 00:05:02,550
混血的后代是优秀的
Hybrid offspring are perfectly fine.

90
00:05:02,660 --> 00:05:04,220
它们更具活力，更健康
There, there viable and healthy.

91
00:05:04,340 --> 00:05:06,990
它们可以繁殖，产下它们的卵
They can, their fertile, so they can lay their own eggs.

92
00:05:07,400 --> 00:05:09,940
这些蝴蝶都有相同的寿命
These butterflies all have the same life-span

93
00:05:10,050 --> 00:05:13,270
都有相同的躲避捕食者的毒性
and the same adaptive poison to ward off predators.

94
00:05:13,640 --> 00:05:18,170
它们的差异只是外在的
The differences appear to be only skin deep.

95
00:05:19,110 --> 00:05:21,500
这和人类一样
It is the same for humans.

96
00:05:22,070 --> 00:05:26,160
种族没有形成生殖隔离
Race poses no biological barriers to mating,

97
00:05:26,500 --> 00:05:31,090
我们的寿命也是相近的
and our life-spans are all very similar,

98
00:05:31,200 --> 00:05:34,060
与我们的人种背景无关
no matter what our ethnic background.

99
00:05:34,170 --> 00:05:38,000
人类，会同蛱蝶一样
Are people, like Heliconius butterflies,

100
00:05:38,110 --> 00:05:41,750
肤色之下的一切都是相同的么？
all the same beneath our different-colored skins?

101
00:05:42,910 --> 00:05:44,500
2000年
In the year 2000,

102
00:05:44,610 --> 00:05:47,300
当克林顿总统宣布
when President Clinton announced the completion

103
00:05:47,410 --> 00:05:48,970
人类基因组计划完成之时
of the Human Genome Project,

104
00:05:49,080 --> 00:05:51,470
答案似乎是“是的”
the answer appeared to be yes.

105
00:05:51,580 --> 00:05:52,670
在遗传意义上
And in genetic terms,

106
00:05:52,780 --> 00:05:54,770
无论人种，所有的人类
all human beings, regardless of race,

107
00:05:54,890 --> 00:05:57,810
超过99.9%是相同的
are more than 99.9% the same.

108
00:05:57,920 --> 00:06:00,480
另外十年的遗传学研究
Another decade of genetic study

109
00:06:00,590 --> 00:06:05,050
将这个比率修正到99.5%
has lowered that number somewhat to 99.5%.

110
00:06:05,160 --> 00:06:08,020
无论你是谁，你来自何方
No matter who you are or where you come from,

111
00:06:08,130 --> 00:06:14,560
只有区区0.5%的基因是你独有的
a mere 0.5% of your genetic code is unique to you.

112
00:06:14,670 --> 00:06:20,670
这0.5%之间能有多大的人种差异呢？
What kinds of racial differences could like in that 0.5%?

113
00:06:20,880 --> 00:06:22,770
可能是相当大
Perhaps plenty.

114
00:06:22,880 --> 00:06:27,740
人类和黑猩猩之间的基因差异也只是3%而已
Human and chimpanzee genomes only differ by about 3%.

115
00:06:27,850 --> 00:06:29,370
这些差异足以
That difference is enough

116
00:06:29,490 --> 00:06:34,490
使我们的大脑彻底变得更大更聪明
to make our brains radically bigger and smarter.

117
00:06:38,360 --> 00:06:41,590
约翰·霍克斯 是一位顶尖的古人类学家
John Hawks is a leading paleoanthropologist

118
00:06:41,700 --> 00:06:44,390
工作在威斯康辛大学
at the University of Wisconsin-Madison.

119
00:06:44,500 --> 00:06:47,360
他研究古人类的骨骼
He studies the bones of ancient humans,

120
00:06:47,470 --> 00:06:49,390
跟踪我们的改变
tracking how we have changed

121
00:06:49,510 --> 00:06:51,530
自从我们的进化路线
since our evolutionary line split off

122
00:06:51,640 --> 00:06:55,870
在六百万年前与黑猩猩分道扬镳以来
from that of the chimp's about 6 million years ago.

123
00:06:55,980 --> 00:06:59,440
回顾我们进化的简史
When we look at a real brief thumbnail of our evolution,

124
00:06:59,550 --> 00:07:00,940
从类人猿开始
we started out as apes

125
00:07:01,050 --> 00:07:02,810
成为直立人
and became upright-walking people

126
00:07:02,920 --> 00:07:04,940
然后进化到制造石器
and then evolved stone-tool manufacture

127
00:07:05,060 --> 00:07:06,310
大脑变得更大
and bigger brains.

128
00:07:06,420 --> 00:07:09,480
这些大事记跨越了数百万年
And those are big events that took millions of years.

129
00:07:09,590 --> 00:07:11,920
但是近年来我大部分工作
But most of my work in the last few years

130
00:07:12,030 --> 00:07:15,250
致力于我们进化的最近部分
has been focused on the very recent part of our evolution --

131
00:07:15,370 --> 00:07:17,320
自从我们离开非洲
our time since we left Africa

132
00:07:17,430 --> 00:07:20,440
进化到现在的人类
and evolved into the people we are today.

133
00:07:22,840 --> 00:07:24,430
科学家们现在同意
Scientists now agree

134
00:07:24,540 --> 00:07:26,300
人种分化开始于
the development of separate races

135
00:07:26,410 --> 00:07:28,830
距今大约五万年前
began around 50,000 years ago,

136
00:07:28,950 --> 00:07:32,200
当现代人迁出非洲之时
when modern humans migrated out of Africa.

137
00:07:32,320 --> 00:07:35,770
从那时起，深肤色一直是
There dark-skinned pigmentation was and remains

138
00:07:35,890 --> 00:07:39,680
抵挡紫外线的防护
a protective force against ultraviolet rays.

139
00:07:39,790 --> 00:07:42,650
当人类向北迁徙
But as humans moved further north,

140
00:07:42,760 --> 00:07:45,720
深肤色阻挡了太多的阳光
dark skin blocked too much sunlight

141
00:07:45,830 --> 00:07:48,750
减少了我们皮肤深处
and reduced the natural production of vitamin D

142
00:07:48,870 --> 00:07:50,420
维生素D的合成
in our deep skin layers.

143
00:07:50,530 --> 00:07:55,330
在欧洲和中国，肤色较浅的人表现得更好
Lighter-skinned people fared better in Europe and China.

144
00:07:55,440 --> 00:07:56,860
对于那些迁徙到
For those who migrated

145
00:07:56,970 --> 00:07:59,400
南美和南印度的人
to South America and Southern India,

146
00:07:59,510 --> 00:08:03,670
深肤色继续为他们防晒
darker skin once again protected them from the sun.

147
00:08:03,780 --> 00:08:05,440
约翰的研究表明
But John's research is showing

148
00:08:05,550 --> 00:08:08,710
此刻的种族划分
that these now-isolated ethnicities

149
00:08:08,820 --> 00:08:12,210
在接下来的几千年来继续演变
continued to evolve over the ensuing millennia

150
00:08:12,320 --> 00:08:14,610
人种之间的差异
and that distinctions between racial groups

151
00:08:14,730 --> 00:08:17,280
确实不仅只在肤色
do go deeper than our skin.

152
00:08:17,390 --> 00:08:19,620
当我研究考古学标本——
When I study archaeological samples

153
00:08:19,730 --> 00:08:22,390
这些出土的头骨
of skulls that have come out of the ground

154
00:08:22,500 --> 00:08:25,790
来自大约五千或一万年前
from 5,000 years ago, from 10,000 years ago,

155
00:08:25,900 --> 00:08:29,960
我们能看到，随着时间前行
we can see many of the changes unfolding in those samples

156
00:08:30,070 --> 00:08:33,330
这些标本有很多演变
as we go forward in time.

157
00:08:33,440 --> 00:08:35,030
脑容量变化了
Brain size changed.

158
00:08:35,150 --> 00:08:36,800
牙齿变化了
Teeth changed.

159
00:08:36,910 --> 00:08:38,870
有几种变化
And then there are some kinds of changes

160
00:08:38,980 --> 00:08:41,240
随着地区有着明显的差异
that are distinct to different regions.

161
00:08:41,350 --> 00:08:42,770
可以通过这些特征
So, there are characteristics

162
00:08:42,890 --> 00:08:44,680
我们能指出
that we can point at in the skull

163
00:08:44,790 --> 00:08:48,580
现代亚洲人和欧洲人头骨的特质
that now characterize Asians versus Europeans.

164
00:08:48,690 --> 00:08:50,920
约翰认为种族的差异
John thinks racial differences

165
00:08:51,030 --> 00:08:54,190
远比骨头要深
run much deeper than our bones.

166
00:08:54,300 --> 00:08:58,260
他认为几乎所有的生物学特征
He believes almost every aspect of our biology changed

167
00:08:58,370 --> 00:09:00,890
都随着人类在地球上的迁徙而改变
as humans migrated around the world,

168
00:09:01,000 --> 00:09:03,370
甚至我们大脑工作的方式
even the way our brains work.

169
00:09:06,670 --> 00:09:09,100
是什么造成了种族的差异
What separates one race from another?

170
00:09:10,210 --> 00:09:11,600
五万年来
In the 50,000 years

171
00:09:11,720 --> 00:09:15,870
自从迁出非洲的一小群人以来
since small groups of people began migrating out of Africa,

172
00:09:15,990 --> 00:09:19,540
我们产生了众多身体的差异
we've developed plenty of physical differences.

173
00:09:19,660 --> 00:09:23,450
但是无形的差异是什么
But what invisible difference might there be?

174
00:09:23,560 --> 00:09:29,300
只是我们DNA漂变了多远？
Just how far apart has our DNA drifted?

175
00:09:38,240 --> 00:09:41,000
古人类学家 约翰·霍克斯
Paleoanthropologist John Hawks

176
00:09:41,110 --> 00:09:44,510
正在追踪过去一千年中我们基因的改变
is tracking changes in our genes over the past millennia.

177
00:09:45,850 --> 00:09:49,340
他发现了令人不安的一些东西
And he's discovered something unsettling.

178
00:09:49,450 --> 00:09:53,190
我们的DNA酷爱赌博
Our DNA loves to gamble.

179
00:09:58,130 --> 00:10:01,650
我们观察DNA改变的方式
So, when we look at the way that DNA changes,

180
00:10:01,770 --> 00:10:04,990
有点像我们的基因沉迷于赌博
it's sort of like our genes are addicted to gambling.

181
00:10:05,100 --> 00:10:08,190
它有四种可能的碱基对，A、C、G和T
It's got four possible base pairs -- A, C, G, and T.

182
00:10:08,310 --> 00:10:11,230
我们这里拿到，四种颜色的筹码
And here we've got, well, four colors of chips.

183
00:10:11,340 --> 00:10:13,730
这就像DNA序列
This is like a DNA sequence,

184
00:10:13,850 --> 00:10:16,750
除了你的DNA要比这长得多
except your DNA would be enormously longer.

185
00:10:19,680 --> 00:10:23,140
进化在这些基因序列上的工作原理……
The way that evolution works on these gene sequences...

186
00:10:23,250 --> 00:10:27,250
当基因序列自我复制时
As this gene sequence is reproducing itself,

187
00:10:27,360 --> 00:10:31,080
一个碱基对可能被换出
one base pair may get swapped out

188
00:10:31,200 --> 00:10:34,920
另一个不同的被放在这里
and a different one put in its place.

189
00:10:35,030 --> 00:10:38,660
当我们逐一复制出许多单独DNA时
As we lay out many individuals' DNA next to each other,

190
00:10:38,770 --> 00:10:41,540
每个单独DNA间 可能表现出差异
those individuals are gonna be different from each other

191
00:10:41,650 --> 00:10:42,650
（差异）在随机的位置上
at random places.

192
00:10:42,760 --> 00:10:44,530
当突变发生时
When a mutation happens,

193
00:10:44,640 --> 00:10:46,800
可能引起大问题
that can cause an enormous problem.

194
00:10:46,910 --> 00:10:48,700
也可以是巨大的进步
Or it could be an enormous advantage.

195
00:10:48,810 --> 00:10:51,240
每代之间
Between every generation,

196
00:10:51,350 --> 00:10:55,440
DNA序列大约会产生60次随机改变
DNA makes about 60 of these random changes to its sequence.

197
00:10:55,550 --> 00:10:57,580
有时，这会使它大赚一笔
Sometimes, it hits the jackpot.

198
00:10:57,690 --> 00:10:59,980
有时，它输得倾家荡产
Sometimes, it goes bust.

199
00:11:00,090 --> 00:11:03,650
大多数情况下，这些突变没有任何影响
Most of the time, these mutations do nothing at all.

200
00:11:03,760 --> 00:11:06,550
所有不致死的改变
But all the changes that don't kill us

201
00:11:06,660 --> 00:11:09,050
都传给了后代
are handed down to the next generation,

202
00:11:09,170 --> 00:11:11,420
如同分子化石
living on like molecular fossils.

203
00:11:11,540 --> 00:11:14,960
这些化石给约翰提供了一种方法
And these fossils give John a way to calculate

204
00:11:15,070 --> 00:11:17,700
来算出特定基因有多古老
how old any particular gene is.

205
00:11:17,810 --> 00:11:20,370
它保留的时间越长
The longer it has been around,

206
00:11:20,480 --> 00:11:24,040
它周围就收集了越多的随机突变
the more random mutations would have collected around it.

207
00:11:24,150 --> 00:11:27,070
重要功能的部分保持不变
The part that's functionally important stays the same,

208
00:11:27,190 --> 00:11:29,740
离重要部分越远
but as you go farther and farther away from that part,

209
00:11:29,850 --> 00:11:31,610
就越有可能
it's more likely to have

210
00:11:31,720 --> 00:11:34,310
和其他序列发生交换
swapped up with another sequence.

211
00:11:34,430 --> 00:11:38,450
没有发生交换部分的长度
It's the length of that part that hasn't been swapped

212
00:11:38,560 --> 00:11:39,650
使我们可以算出
that gives us an idea

213
00:11:39,760 --> 00:11:41,750
它在这里已经存在了多久
of how long it's been around in the population.

214
00:11:41,870 --> 00:11:44,290
因为周围的基因越长
Because the longer the gene has been around,

215
00:11:44,400 --> 00:11:47,390
就越有可能发生随机改变
the more likely it is that we'll have these random changes.

216
00:11:47,510 --> 00:11:48,990
约翰和他的同事
When John and his colleagues

217
00:11:49,110 --> 00:11:51,700
将这种基因年代确定技术
used this gene-dating technique

218
00:11:51,810 --> 00:11:55,900
应用在欧洲、亚洲和非洲人身上时
in populations native to Europe, Asia, and Africa,

219
00:11:56,010 --> 00:11:57,970
他们大吃一惊
they were in for a big surprise.

220
00:11:58,080 --> 00:12:01,670
许多基因比想象的要年轻得多
Many genes were much younger than they'd expected.

221
00:12:01,790 --> 00:12:04,540
我们发现它们中的许多证明了
What we discovered was that lots of them

222
00:12:04,660 --> 00:12:07,480
适应变化是非常之快
showed evidence of really fast adaptive changes.

223
00:12:07,590 --> 00:12:10,380
我们研究大约2000个地区
We're talking about 2,000 places

224
00:12:10,490 --> 00:12:13,750
世界上许多地区
that, in one part of the world or another,

225
00:12:13,860 --> 00:12:16,690
刚刚经历了最近的适应
have undergone really recent adaptation.

226
00:12:16,800 --> 00:12:19,660
对于数字是如此之大，我们非常惊讶
We were pretty surprised to find that the number was so large.

227
00:12:19,770 --> 00:12:21,530
约翰原以为
John had expected to find

228
00:12:21,640 --> 00:12:24,730
只有一小部分人类基因
that just a fraction of a percent of human genes

229
00:12:24,840 --> 00:12:27,270
能展现出最近突变的迹象
would show signs of recent mutation.

230
00:12:27,380 --> 00:12:31,600
但事实是，他发现大约7%的基因
But instead, he discovered that about 7% of our genes

231
00:12:31,720 --> 00:12:33,710
突变成了新的形式
have mutated to new forms

232
00:12:33,820 --> 00:12:36,270
在过去的一两万年之间
in the last 10,000 to 20,000 years.

233
00:12:36,390 --> 00:12:41,150
有些基因只在特定种族中才被发现
Some of these genes are only found in certain racial groups.

234
00:12:41,260 --> 00:12:44,920
这些突变不仅仅关乎皮肤颜色
These mutations are not just related to skin color

235
00:12:45,030 --> 00:12:47,120
和身体外貌
and physical appearance.

236
00:12:47,230 --> 00:12:49,720
改变很深
The changes go far deeper.

237
00:12:49,830 --> 00:12:52,360
恐怕最明显的证据
Probably the most obvious examples

238
00:12:52,470 --> 00:12:55,360
就是对于一种新的疾病
are the examples where there's a disease that's new

239
00:12:55,470 --> 00:12:58,960
某些地区的人类已经有了抵抗力
that some people have developed resistance strategies to.

240
00:12:59,080 --> 00:13:00,800
例如，疟疾
So, malaria, for example,

241
00:13:00,910 --> 00:13:03,670
它的人类病原体
is a disease that has been around for about 5,000 years

242
00:13:03,780 --> 00:13:05,370
存在了5000多年
as a human pathogen.

243
00:13:05,480 --> 00:13:09,270
在此期间，南亚和非洲的人群
Over that time, populations in South Asia, in Africa,

244
00:13:09,390 --> 00:13:13,510
对于这种疾病已经有了适应性
have developed new adaptations to this particular disease.

245
00:13:13,620 --> 00:13:16,150
约翰怀疑还有另一种力量
But John suspects there is another force

246
00:13:16,260 --> 00:13:18,050
驱动着基因变化——
driving these genetic changes --

247
00:13:18,160 --> 00:13:20,490
我们的文明
our civilization.

248
00:13:20,600 --> 00:13:22,350
这种想法在他与大部分进化学家之间
It is an idea that puts him at odds

249
00:13:22,470 --> 00:13:24,920
引起了争执
with most evolutionary scientists,

250
00:13:25,040 --> 00:13:27,630
包括他们的前辈
including the father of them all --

251
00:13:27,740 --> 00:13:29,330
查尔斯·达尔文
Charles Darwin.

252
00:13:29,440 --> 00:13:33,530
达尔文认为自然的敌对力量
Darwin talked about the hostile forces of nature,

253
00:13:33,640 --> 00:13:37,870
阳光的暴晒，冬季的寒冷
the sun beating down on us, the cold of the winter,

254
00:13:37,980 --> 00:13:40,470
都会改变我们
and these would change us,

255
00:13:40,580 --> 00:13:43,440
因为我们必须适应那些环境
because we had to adapt to those environments.

256
00:13:43,550 --> 00:13:46,410
但是在人类进化中，我们可以发明
And in human evolution, we could invent things.

257
00:13:46,520 --> 00:13:50,220
我们可以改变我们的行为和文化
We could change our behavior and our culture to insulate us,

258
00:13:50,330 --> 00:13:52,180
使我们和那些之间有缓冲和隔离
to buffer us from those things.

259
00:13:52,300 --> 00:13:55,920
这种想法即 人类不必作出生理的改变
And so the idea was that humans didn't have to change biology

260
00:13:56,030 --> 00:13:58,430
用以适应新环境
in order to adapt to new places.

261
00:14:00,500 --> 00:14:04,060
查尔斯·达尔文认为
Charles Darwin's argument was

262
00:14:04,180 --> 00:14:06,800
耕种、房子、衣服
that farming, houses, clothes,

263
00:14:06,910 --> 00:14:09,370
会阻止我们基因的改变和进化
should stop our genes from changing and evolving,

264
00:14:09,480 --> 00:14:13,000
由于我们开始保护自己，离开环境危害
because we began protecting ourselves from our environments.

265
00:14:13,120 --> 00:14:16,240
但是约翰的研究显示
But John's research shows

266
00:14:16,350 --> 00:14:19,710
在世界不同之处
that the different ways groups of humans chose to live

267
00:14:19,820 --> 00:14:22,180
选择不同生活方式的群体
in different parts of the world

268
00:14:22,290 --> 00:14:24,850
也会产生基因的改变
could actually have driven genetic changes.

269
00:14:24,960 --> 00:14:26,350
可能对于文化影响进化
Probably the best example

270
00:14:26,460 --> 00:14:29,850
最好的例子就是喝牛奶
of how culture has influenced our evolution is milk drinking.

271
00:14:29,970 --> 00:14:33,090
现在，一个成年哺乳动物
Now, it's not normal for adult mammals

272
00:14:33,200 --> 00:14:35,090
喝奶是不正常的
to have access to milk.

273
00:14:35,210 --> 00:14:37,760
想像一头公牛试图喝奶
If you think about a bull trying to get milk

274
00:14:37,880 --> 00:14:39,540
在母牛的乳房下
up from underneath the cow's udder...

275
00:14:40,680 --> 00:14:42,480
自然界中没有这种现象
...it just doesn't work in nature.

276
00:14:44,250 --> 00:14:46,440
有许多人
There have been many populations

277
00:14:46,550 --> 00:14:49,140
已经适应了各种动物乳制品
that have adopted dairy animals of different kinds.

278
00:14:49,250 --> 00:14:52,610
他们中已有人通过新的突变
Five of them have developed new mutations

279
00:14:52,720 --> 00:14:54,750
赋予了他们作为成年人
that give them, as adults,

280
00:14:54,860 --> 00:14:57,850
仍具有消化乳糖的能力
the ability to digest the sugar in this milk.

281
00:14:57,960 --> 00:15:00,950
在北欧，这是一种相当普遍的突变
In northern Europe, this is a really common mutation.

282
00:15:01,070 --> 00:15:03,890
但是与世界其他地区相比，比如中国
But if you go to come parts of the world, like China,

283
00:15:04,000 --> 00:15:07,070
人们就不怎么能喝牛奶
it's very rare for people to be able to drink milk.

284
00:15:08,610 --> 00:15:11,830
事实上，消化牛奶的能力
But in fact, it's the ability to digest this milk,

285
00:15:11,940 --> 00:15:14,600
我们称之为 乳糖耐受性
the lactase persistence, as we call it,

286
00:15:14,710 --> 00:15:17,170
是一个怪异的突变版本
which is the weird, mutant version of this.

287
00:15:17,280 --> 00:15:19,440
它在人群中发生
And it's all happened in populations

288
00:15:19,550 --> 00:15:21,770
是由于他们改变了文化
because they've changed their culture.

289
00:15:21,890 --> 00:15:23,680
就像气候一样
Just as the climates

290
00:15:23,790 --> 00:15:26,310
产生了不同地区的种族
where different ethnic groups lived varied,

291
00:15:26,420 --> 00:15:29,250
他们社会的规则和习惯也一样
so did the rules and habits of their societies.

292
00:15:29,360 --> 00:15:32,450
他们的DNA被迫适应
And their DNA was forced to adapt.

293
00:15:32,560 --> 00:15:34,390
如果你是人类
If you're a human,

294
00:15:34,500 --> 00:15:37,890
你必须生存，并和其他人共处，日复一日
you have to survive and deal with other people every day

295
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:39,320
为了繁衍生息
in order to reproduce.

296
00:15:39,440 --> 00:15:42,760
那便组成了养育你的文化和环境
And that makes culture your environment.

297
00:15:42,870 --> 00:15:45,330
遗传适应
Genetic adaptations

298
00:15:45,440 --> 00:15:48,500
甚至可能已改变了我们大脑的工作方式
may even have altered the way our brains work.

299
00:15:48,610 --> 00:15:50,340
约翰和他的同事
John and his colleagues

300
00:15:50,450 --> 00:15:53,270
发现了约100种基因突变
have discovered around 100 mutations

301
00:15:53,380 --> 00:15:55,370
控制着大脑化学物质
in genes controlling brain chemistry

302
00:15:55,490 --> 00:15:56,610
这些突变一直在发生
that have taken place

303
00:15:56,720 --> 00:15:59,740
自从人类迁出非洲以来
since humanity migrated from Africa.

304
00:15:59,860 --> 00:16:05,220
其中的一个，称为DRD4的遗传性变型
One of them, a genetic variant called DRD4,

305
00:16:05,330 --> 00:16:09,750
有可能触发了最初的迁徙
may even have triggered that migration in the first place.

306
00:16:09,870 --> 00:16:11,120
它和注意力缺陷多动障碍（多动症）有关
It's linked to ADHD,

307
00:16:11,240 --> 00:16:14,090
我们研究多动症患者
because when we study patients who have ADHD,

308
00:16:14,210 --> 00:16:17,260
他们更有可能拥有这个基因
they have a greater chance of having this gene.

309
00:16:17,370 --> 00:16:18,730
一个可能的原因是
One possible reason is

310
00:16:18,840 --> 00:16:21,370
它使得人们更喜欢运动
that it made people more likely to move.

311
00:16:21,480 --> 00:16:24,800
DED4突变
The DRD4 mutation is most commonly found

312
00:16:24,920 --> 00:16:28,440
在非洲以外的人口中更多见
in populations that live outside of Africa.

313
00:16:28,550 --> 00:16:31,840
它出现于约五万年前
It appeared around 50,000 years ago.

314
00:16:31,960 --> 00:16:34,510
它被称为“迁徙基因”
It has been called "the migration gene,"

315
00:16:34,630 --> 00:16:37,320
迅速转移注意力
because traits like rapidly shifting focus

316
00:16:37,430 --> 00:16:38,950
和快速移动的特质
and quick movements

317
00:16:39,060 --> 00:16:43,450
当我们祖先迁徙的时候非常有用
could have been very useful when our ancestors were on the move.

318
00:16:43,570 --> 00:16:47,160
尽管迅速转移注意力 显得毫无用处
Even though short attention now appears less useful

319
00:16:47,270 --> 00:16:49,790
尤其在长期伏案的现代社会
in our modern, sedentary society.

320
00:16:49,910 --> 00:16:53,130
DRD4是关于
DRD4 is the best example of a gene

321
00:16:53,240 --> 00:16:55,600
最近选择的基因 影响行为表现的
that is clearly recently selected

322
00:16:55,710 --> 00:16:57,470
最好的例子
and has behavioral impacts.

323
00:16:57,580 --> 00:16:59,200
它以某种方式影响大脑工作
It affects the brain in some way.

324
00:16:59,320 --> 00:17:00,570
还有其他的
But there are others.

325
00:17:00,680 --> 00:17:02,240
我们不知道它们有什么用
And we don't know what they do,

326
00:17:02,350 --> 00:17:04,980
但是我们知道它们在大脑中表达
but we can say that they're expressed in the brain,

327
00:17:05,090 --> 00:17:08,550
它们很可能以某种方式影响我们的行为表现
they're related to our behavior in some way, potentially.

328
00:17:08,660 --> 00:17:10,620
但我们不知道那些变化是为了什么
But we don't know what those changes are for.

329
00:17:10,730 --> 00:17:15,150
遗传变化影响大脑的概念
The notion that genetic changes affecting the brain

330
00:17:15,270 --> 00:17:16,590
事实上可能会使得
might actually underpin

331
00:17:16,700 --> 00:17:19,020
分居在全球各地的人类
the spread of humanity across the globe

332
00:17:19,140 --> 00:17:21,660
产生一个令人不安的问题
leads to an unsettling question.

333
00:17:21,770 --> 00:17:24,360
是否有些种族已经进化
Might some races have evolved

334
00:17:24,480 --> 00:17:27,670
而变得比其他种族更聪明了?
to become more intelligent than others?

335
00:17:27,780 --> 00:17:31,140
这是极具争议的概念
It is a highly divisive notion,

336
00:17:31,250 --> 00:17:34,340
一些科学家探索这个问题
but some scientists are probing the issue,

337
00:17:34,450 --> 00:17:38,850
他们的结论引起了愤怒
and their conclusions have triggered outrage.

338
00:17:41,830 --> 00:17:44,020
种族
Race.

339
00:17:44,130 --> 00:17:47,750
一个带着强烈感情色彩的词
It is a word that stirs powerful emotions.

340
00:17:47,870 --> 00:17:49,150
一些科学家认为
Some scientists think

341
00:17:49,270 --> 00:17:52,220
我们之间的遗传学差异如此之小
the genetic differences between us are so small

342
00:17:52,340 --> 00:17:55,490
种族这个词 甚至没有意义
that the word "race" doesn't even make sense.

343
00:17:55,610 --> 00:17:59,830
但懂得进化是如何形成种族
But understanding how evolution has shaped the races

344
00:17:59,940 --> 00:18:01,730
接下来会怎么做
and continues to do so

345
00:18:01,850 --> 00:18:04,780
具有重要的科学价值
is of great scientific importance.

346
00:18:07,020 --> 00:18:10,610
将人类和其他动物区分开的特质
The trait that sets humans apart from all other species

347
00:18:10,720 --> 00:18:14,110
是我们难以置信的复杂大脑
is our incredibly complex brain.

348
00:18:14,230 --> 00:18:17,850
不同种族的大脑会有区别吗？
But could the brains of different races be different?

349
00:18:17,960 --> 00:18:21,890
它们会有不同的智力吗？
Could they have different intelligences?

350
00:18:22,000 --> 00:18:25,160
著名心理学家 斯坦利·科伦
Renowned psychologist Stanley Coren

351
00:18:25,270 --> 00:18:28,860
数年来，在研究智力的差异
has spent years studying intelligence differences --

352
00:18:28,970 --> 00:18:31,560
不过不在人种之间
not among different races of people,

353
00:18:31,680 --> 00:18:35,170
而在不同品种的狗狗之间
but in different breeds of dogs.

354
00:18:35,280 --> 00:18:37,740
狗是基因工程的奇迹
Dogs are a marvel of genetic engineering,

355
00:18:37,850 --> 00:18:40,540
仅仅由于我们保持了品种的区分
simply because we've kept the breeds separate.

356
00:18:40,650 --> 00:18:47,410
你知道，我的祖父母来自托拉维亚、立陶宛、俄罗斯
You know, my grandparents were Latvia, Lithuania, Russia,

357
00:18:47,530 --> 00:18:49,480
但是我的金毛猎犬
but I take a golden retriever,

358
00:18:49,590 --> 00:18:52,820
它的祖父是纯种的金毛猎犬
and his granddaddy was a purebred golden retriever,

359
00:18:52,930 --> 00:18:55,990
它的曾祖父也是，如此等等
and his great-granddaddy was, and so on and so forth.

360
00:18:56,100 --> 00:18:58,090
所以，基因上噪声很少
So, there's less noise in the genome.

361
00:18:58,200 --> 00:19:01,860
至少在一万四千年间
For at least 14,000 years,

362
00:19:01,970 --> 00:19:05,630
人类系统地发展了狗的进化
humans have systematically shaped the evolution of dogs,

363
00:19:05,740 --> 00:19:08,430
改变它们，以适合我们的需求
changing them to fit our needs.

364
00:19:08,550 --> 00:19:11,270
斯坦利 测量了一百多个品种的
Stanley has measured the intelligence

365
00:19:11,380 --> 00:19:13,070
狗狗的智力
of more than 100 breeds.

366
00:19:13,180 --> 00:19:17,410
所以我们能说，不同品种的狗之间
So, we can get dogs which, because of their breeding,

367
00:19:17,520 --> 00:19:20,450
有智力方面的差异
differ in terms of their intelligence.

368
00:19:20,560 --> 00:19:23,320
如果有一个不到一岁半的孩子
If you take a young child below 18 months of age

369
00:19:23,430 --> 00:19:25,450
你把毛巾放在他的头上
and you put a towel over his head,

370
00:19:25,560 --> 00:19:27,530
他觉得，世界消失了
he thinks that the world has gone away,

371
00:19:27,640 --> 00:19:28,640
他还坐在那里
and he sits there.

372
00:19:28,760 --> 00:19:31,390
所以，如果萌塔娜的智力超过
So, if Montana has a mental ability

373
00:19:31,500 --> 00:19:33,530
一岁半的小孩
beyond an 18-month-old human,

374
00:19:33,640 --> 00:19:35,630
萌塔娜会很快把头上的毛巾
Montana should throw the towel off of his head

375
00:19:35,740 --> 00:19:36,760
扔掉
very quickly.

376
00:19:36,880 --> 00:19:38,810
萌塔娜，预备，开始～
Are you ready, Montana? Go.

377
00:19:44,210 --> 00:19:47,440
斯坦利的测试结果 使他确信
Stanley's test results have convinced him

378
00:19:47,550 --> 00:19:50,980
不同品种的犬类之间
that there are big differences in canine intelligence

379
00:19:51,090 --> 00:19:53,480
智力有很大差异
from breed to breed.

380
00:19:53,590 --> 00:19:55,250
谁把灯关了？
Where has the world gone?

381
00:19:55,360 --> 00:19:58,220
好吧，好狗狗～
Okay, what a good dog.

382
00:19:58,330 --> 00:20:00,690
这是一只比格犬
And then I've got a beagle.

383
00:20:00,800 --> 00:20:04,220
它的工作是陪我的孙子玩
And his job is to amuse my grandchildren.

384
00:20:04,340 --> 00:20:06,830
狗狗的智力
As dog intelligence goes,

385
00:20:06,940 --> 00:20:09,030
比格犬排在倒数第七
beagles are seven from the bottom.

386
00:20:09,140 --> 00:20:11,930
所以，比格犬
So, this bench that I'm sitting on right now

387
00:20:12,040 --> 00:20:13,970
比我坐的凳子还笨 =_=#
is more trainable than a beagle.

388
00:20:14,080 --> 00:20:16,940
最聪明的狗
The top dogs in terms of intelligence

389
00:20:17,050 --> 00:20:20,510
是博德牧羊犬，接下来是贵妇犬
are the Border Collie, followed by the poodle.

390
00:20:20,620 --> 00:20:22,840
有人会说，“贵妇犬？就凭它那样子？”
Some people say, "the poodle? That's a froufrou dog."

391
00:20:22,950 --> 00:20:25,540
不，贵妇犬是猎犬，好不好
No, the poodle is a retriever, okay?

392
00:20:25,660 --> 00:20:28,580
再说，又不是它自己想剪那种傻叉发型
And he didn't ask for that silly haircut.

393
00:20:28,690 --> 00:20:32,150
如果某些品种的狗比其他的更聪明
If some breeds of dogs are smarter than others,

394
00:20:32,260 --> 00:20:33,650
那么对于在狗链另一端，
why could that not be true

395
00:20:33,770 --> 00:20:36,920
牵着狗的那种动物，何尝不同呢？
for the animals at the other end of the leash?

396
00:20:37,040 --> 00:20:42,130
德拉华大学的社会学家 琳达·高德弗逊
University of Delaware sociologist Linda Gottfredson

397
00:20:42,240 --> 00:20:45,000
分析了过去二十年间的
has been analyzing I.Q. test scores

398
00:20:45,110 --> 00:20:46,930
智商测试结果
for the past two decades.

399
00:20:47,050 --> 00:20:52,000
她声称，他们揭示了 智力、遗传学和种族之间
She claims they reveal a subtle but measurable link

400
00:20:52,120 --> 00:20:55,940
微妙但可度量的联系
between intelligence, genetics, and race.

401
00:20:56,050 --> 00:20:59,310
对于智力这种非常复杂的特质
For very complex traits like intelligence,

402
00:20:59,420 --> 00:21:02,210
许多基因都有小的影响
many genes have small effect,

403
00:21:02,330 --> 00:21:05,320
可能会促使一个人 这样或那样
may push a person this way or that.

404
00:21:05,430 --> 00:21:09,820
那是相当相当地难以发现
And they're really, really hard to find.

405
00:21:09,930 --> 00:21:12,190
个体的智商
Individual I.Q.s

406
00:21:12,300 --> 00:21:16,530
就像海滩上沙粒一样多种多样
are as diverse as the grains of sand on a beach.

407
00:21:16,640 --> 00:21:20,730
但琳达认为，在这一片嘈杂中有共同的模式
But Linda believes there are patterns in this noise,

408
00:21:20,850 --> 00:21:24,600
我们基因决定的模式
patterns that depend on our genes.

409
00:21:24,720 --> 00:21:26,710
每个父母都知道
As any parent knows,

410
00:21:26,820 --> 00:21:28,270
兄弟姐妹们看起来不一样
brothers and sisters look different.

411
00:21:28,390 --> 00:21:30,810
他们往往有不同的个性
They often have different personalities,

412
00:21:30,920 --> 00:21:34,050
他们的智力水平通常也有差异
and they often have different levels of intelligence.

413
00:21:34,160 --> 00:21:37,680
兄弟姐妹间平均差异
The average difference between siblings

414
00:21:37,800 --> 00:21:40,350
大约是12个智商分值
is 12 I.Q. points.

415
00:21:40,470 --> 00:21:43,790
如果将行走在沙滩上的人们 随机相比
If you compare to random people walking on the beach,

416
00:21:43,900 --> 00:21:47,260
你可能问到，他们之间的差异是多少？
you might ask, "Well, how different are those people?"

417
00:21:47,370 --> 00:21:52,100
陌生人之间平均差异是17个分值
On the average, strangers differ by 17 I.Q. points.

418
00:21:52,210 --> 00:21:55,930
所以，生物学上的兄弟姐妹之间的差异
So, you see that biological brothers and sisters

419
00:21:56,050 --> 00:21:58,900
是街上随机陌生人之间
are 2/3 as different on the average

420
00:21:59,020 --> 00:22:01,770
差异的大约2/3
as random strangers on the street.

421
00:22:01,890 --> 00:22:06,510
陌生人之间基因差异更大
The genes of strangers vary more than those of siblings,

422
00:22:06,620 --> 00:22:11,080
琳达认为，（没有血缘关系）就是他们智商差异更大的原因
and Linda argues this is why their I.Q.s vary more.

423
00:22:11,200 --> 00:22:13,590
她对数据的解释
Her interpretation of this data

424
00:22:13,700 --> 00:22:15,690
给了她一个有争议的概念
has led her to a controversial notion,

425
00:22:15,800 --> 00:22:18,260
即 种族之间的基因差异
that the genetic differences between races

426
00:22:18,370 --> 00:22:19,790
可能会导致
might lead to differences

427
00:22:19,900 --> 00:22:23,900
种族之间平均智力的差异
in the average intelligence of those races.

428
00:22:24,010 --> 00:22:25,800
我猜测
I guess there would be

429
00:22:25,910 --> 00:22:28,270
人类多样性有两条准则
two rules about human diversity.

430
00:22:28,380 --> 00:22:34,340
其一是 大量的变异出现在
One is that there's lots of variation

431
00:22:34,450 --> 00:22:36,110
所有的群体之中
within all groups,

432
00:22:36,220 --> 00:22:41,480
而且，群体之间也会有层次
but there's also a gradation between the groups

433
00:22:41,590 --> 00:22:44,820
所以这里有再发的
so that there are recurring

434
00:22:44,930 --> 00:22:47,990
有时会是大的平均差异
and sometimes large average differences

435
00:22:48,100 --> 00:22:49,920
在不同的人种之间
between racial ethnic groups.

436
00:22:50,030 --> 00:22:52,920
每个群体中人的智商分值
I.Q. scores in any group of people

437
00:22:53,040 --> 00:22:55,190
都表现为钟形曲线
are spread across a bell curve

438
00:22:55,310 --> 00:23:00,430
分布在70分到130分之间
ranging from a score of about 70 to about 130.

439
00:23:00,550 --> 00:23:03,240
两端都会有异常值
There are outliers on either end,

440
00:23:03,350 --> 00:23:06,970
但大多数人都集中在平均值附近
but most people are clustered around an average.

441
00:23:07,090 --> 00:23:10,710
智商的平均值 在不同人种之间
There are differences between racial ethnic groups

442
00:23:10,820 --> 00:23:14,150
存在差异
on the average in I.Q.

443
00:23:14,260 --> 00:23:18,980
白人的平均智商被人为设置为100
The average white I.Q. is arbitrarily set at 100.

444
00:23:19,100 --> 00:23:23,390
美国和其他西方国家的黑人
Blacks in the United States and in many other western countries

445
00:23:23,500 --> 00:23:25,620
的平均值为85
average 85.

446
00:23:25,740 --> 00:23:29,560
西班牙裔的平均值大约在80
Hispanics -- the average would be about 80.

447
00:23:29,670 --> 00:23:33,100
美洲原住民大约也在这个等级
Native Americans around that level.

448
00:23:33,210 --> 00:23:37,740
日裔和华裔美国人
And then Japanese and Chinese Americans

449
00:23:37,850 --> 00:23:39,940
超过了白人的平均值
above the white average.

450
00:23:40,050 --> 00:23:45,210
德系犹太人大约在110到115
And then Ashkenazi Jews probably around 110, 115.

451
00:23:45,320 --> 00:23:49,250
琳达的研究使她被科学驱逐
Linda's research has made her a scientific outcast.

452
00:23:49,360 --> 00:23:54,850
她甚至被称为种族主义者，虽然她声明否认
She's even been called a racist, a claim she denies.

453
00:23:54,970 --> 00:23:58,020
批评者认为，智商测试的结果
Her critics argue that I.Q. tests results

454
00:23:58,140 --> 00:24:01,390
被社会经济因素严重歪曲
are heavily skewed by socioeconomic factors.

455
00:24:01,510 --> 00:24:04,960
童年时期的营养，医疗保健的获取
Childhood nutrition and access to healthcare

456
00:24:05,080 --> 00:24:08,100
在不同种族之间差别非常广泛
can vary widely between different racial groups.

457
00:24:08,210 --> 00:24:10,400
如果你居住在
If you live in a good neighborhood

458
00:24:10,520 --> 00:24:12,000
拥有好学校的好街区
with well-funded schools,

459
00:24:12,120 --> 00:24:14,270
你可能会习惯于
you are more likely to be accustomed

460
00:24:14,390 --> 00:24:16,740
智商测试中的学术环境
to the academic setting of an I.Q. test.

461
00:24:16,850 --> 00:24:18,740
如果你居住在这样的街区
And if you live in those neighborhoods,

462
00:24:18,860 --> 00:24:22,180
你更可能是亚裔或者白人
you are more likely to be Asian or White.

463
00:24:22,290 --> 00:24:23,950
也有人关心
There are also concerns

464
00:24:24,060 --> 00:24:27,820
测试问题是否有文化偏见
over whether the test questions have a cultural bias,

465
00:24:27,930 --> 00:24:29,420
一个反映偏见的事实是
a bias reflected by the fact

466
00:24:29,530 --> 00:24:34,530
白人的平均智商被设置为100
that it's the white I.Q. average that's set to 100.

467
00:24:34,640 --> 00:24:38,430
一个种族会否比其他的更聪明？
Is one race smarter than another?

468
00:24:38,540 --> 00:24:41,730
去决议你如何定义聪明
Depends on what you mean by smart.

469
00:24:41,850 --> 00:24:43,400
智商分数能否预言
Could I.Q. scores predict

470
00:24:43,510 --> 00:24:46,040
例如毕加索这样的大艺术家
the greatness of an artist like Picasso

471
00:24:46,150 --> 00:24:49,310
或者甘地这样的政治领袖？
or of a political leader like Gandhi?

472
00:24:49,420 --> 00:24:52,780
智商是一个狭隘的观念
I.Q. is a narrow obsession.

473
00:24:52,890 --> 00:24:55,680
没有两个人想法是完全相同的
No two people think the same way,

474
00:24:55,790 --> 00:24:58,020
这与他们的种族无关
regardless of their race.

475
00:24:58,130 --> 00:25:00,350
所以，提出新问题
So, here's a new question.

476
00:25:00,470 --> 00:25:04,390
如果此刻，不同种族的大脑是相似的
If the brains of the races are similar now,

477
00:25:04,500 --> 00:25:06,730
会一直是这样吗？
will that always be true?

478
00:25:06,840 --> 00:25:09,130
我们仍在进化中
We are still evolving.

479
00:25:09,240 --> 00:25:11,960
会否有一天，我们的大脑会变得具有
Could our brains one day become as different

480
00:25:12,080 --> 00:25:15,810
像博德牧羊犬和贝高犬 那样的差异吗？
as those of Border Collies and beagles?

481
00:25:17,710 --> 00:25:21,710
进化给人类提供了一种难以置信的资产
Evolution has given human beings one incredible asset --

482
00:25:22,720 --> 00:25:25,440
卓越的神经细胞网络
the remarkable network of nerve cells

483
00:25:25,550 --> 00:25:28,180
正在你的脑袋中蜂鸣
buzzing around inside our heads.

484
00:25:28,290 --> 00:25:31,710
三磅重的软组织的生长
The growth of these three pounds of soft tissue

485
00:25:31,830 --> 00:25:33,880
使人类智慧
catapulted human intelligence

486
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:36,890
远远超越于其他物种之上
to a level far above the other species.

487
00:25:37,000 --> 00:25:39,990
它还能走多远？
How much further can it grow?

488
00:25:40,100 --> 00:25:43,290
我们最终能否进化出一种超级种族
Will we eventually evolve into a superior race

489
00:25:43,400 --> 00:25:46,640
超级智慧人类
of super-intelligent humans?

490
00:25:56,520 --> 00:25:58,410
神经科学教授 西蒙·劳克林
Neuroscience Professor Simon Laughlin

491
00:25:58,520 --> 00:26:02,580
在剑桥大学 研究大脑的能量/能力
from the University of Cambridge studies brain power.

492
00:26:02,690 --> 00:26:04,310
他测试
He tests the limits

493
00:26:04,430 --> 00:26:07,750
大脑和神经细胞能力的极限
of what brains and their nerve cells can do,

494
00:26:07,860 --> 00:26:11,190
通过观察它们要烧掉多少燃料
all the while watching how much fuel they guzzle.

495
00:26:11,300 --> 00:26:13,560
我关心大脑表现的
So, I'm interested in what the physical limits

496
00:26:13,670 --> 00:26:15,120
物理极限
to the performance of brains are

497
00:26:15,240 --> 00:26:19,490
以及是什么决定了大脑的处理能力
and in what determines the processing power of brains.

498
00:26:19,610 --> 00:26:22,700
西蒙 创立了一种独特的方法
Simon has developed a unique way to see

499
00:26:22,810 --> 00:26:26,270
观察活体大脑正在处理多少信息
just how much information living brains are processing

500
00:26:26,380 --> 00:26:28,970
并同时记录
while simultaneously keeping track

501
00:26:29,080 --> 00:26:31,510
他们消耗了多少能量
of how much energy they are using.

502
00:26:31,620 --> 00:26:34,010
他观察人类大脑的窗户
His window into the human brain

503
00:26:34,120 --> 00:26:38,410
是通过苍蝇球形眼睛
is through the bulbous eyes of flies.

504
00:26:38,530 --> 00:26:40,180
一个更简单的系统
They're a simpler system,

505
00:26:40,290 --> 00:26:42,320
好比先看一个便携式计算器
so it's like looking at a pocket calculator

506
00:26:42,430 --> 00:26:44,750
在你试图逐步弄懂
before you work up to actually trying to understand

507
00:26:44,870 --> 00:26:46,390
一个真正的大型电脑之前
a really big computer.

508
00:26:46,500 --> 00:26:49,460
苍蝇大脑工作的基本原理
The basic principles by which the fly's brain operates

509
00:26:49,570 --> 00:26:50,690
和我们的是相同的
is the same as ours,

510
00:26:50,810 --> 00:26:53,330
但是它们更容易开展工作
but they're much easier to work with,

511
00:26:53,440 --> 00:26:56,330
而且 我们对它们做了什么 了解得更多
and we have a much more complete understanding of what they do.

512
00:26:56,440 --> 00:27:00,770
西蒙测量苍蝇大脑的能力
Simon's ability to measure the performance of fly brains

513
00:27:00,880 --> 00:27:06,320
归功于这些昆虫最令人讨厌的特征
is all thanks to this insect's most bothersome characteristic.

514
00:27:08,290 --> 00:27:10,480
任何一个试图拍苍蝇的人
Anybody who's tried to swat a fly knows

515
00:27:10,590 --> 00:27:12,650
都知道它们擅长检测运动
that they're very good at detecting movement.

516
00:27:12,760 --> 00:27:16,350
为了做到这一点 苍蝇们必须能够
And to do that, they have to be able to respond

517
00:27:16,460 --> 00:27:19,550
对迅速变化的光线作出响应
to very rapid and fast changes in light.

518
00:27:19,670 --> 00:27:22,160
事实上，检测光线
In fact, detecting light

519
00:27:22,270 --> 00:27:25,360
是苍蝇大脑完成的主要任务
is most of what a fly's brain does.

520
00:27:25,470 --> 00:27:29,500
西蒙的实验室准备了两种苍蝇——
Simon's lab is stocked with two species --

521
00:27:29,610 --> 00:27:32,370
有着大而突出眼睛的绿头苍蝇
the blowfly, with large, bulging eyes,

522
00:27:32,480 --> 00:27:37,210
和小果蝇，它们的眼睛要小得多
and the diminutive fruit fly, whose eyes are much smaller.

523
00:27:37,320 --> 00:27:40,310
他和他的团队将微电极
He and his team fit microelectrodes

524
00:27:40,420 --> 00:27:42,580
植入苍蝇的神经细胞
into the fly's nerve cells,

525
00:27:42,690 --> 00:27:45,750
然后把它们置于闪光灯前
then they expose them to a flickering light

526
00:27:45,860 --> 00:27:49,820
记录它们的处理能力
to record their processing power.

527
00:27:49,930 --> 00:27:52,250
细胞对光作出相应
So, the cell responds to the light

528
00:27:52,370 --> 00:27:54,760
通过改变它们的 膜电位
by changing its membrane potential.

529
00:27:54,870 --> 00:27:56,760
然后我们处理这些信号
And then we can process those signals

530
00:27:56,870 --> 00:28:00,260
算出它们还有多少信息
to work out how much information they contain.

531
00:28:00,370 --> 00:28:02,830
西蒙发现，一只果蝇
Simon discovered that while the fruit fly

532
00:28:02,940 --> 00:28:06,170
可以看到部分光的改变
is able to see some of the changes in light,

533
00:28:06,280 --> 00:28:10,470
而绿头苍蝇甚至可以注意到最微小的闪光
the blowfly picks up on even the most minute flicker.

534
00:28:10,580 --> 00:28:12,410
从绿头苍蝇的球状眼睛中
The blowfly's bulbous eyeballs

535
00:28:12,520 --> 00:28:15,910
涌出大量的神经数据
spew out a huge quantity of neural data,

536
00:28:16,020 --> 00:28:19,910
每分钟的数据 可以存满一个1GB的
enough to fill up a one-gigabyte memory stick

537
00:28:20,030 --> 00:28:21,350
U盘
every minute.

538
00:28:21,460 --> 00:28:24,850
但是绿头苍蝇并非全部优秀
But there's a downside for the blowfly.

539
00:28:24,970 --> 00:28:26,390
绿头苍蝇
So, the blowfly picks up

540
00:28:26,500 --> 00:28:29,890
每秒钟获取的数据量大约是果蝇的五倍
about five times as many bits per second as the fruit fly,

541
00:28:30,000 --> 00:28:32,890
但由于它的高性能
but because it has a much higher performance,

542
00:28:33,010 --> 00:28:34,200
每比特信息
those bits of information,

543
00:28:34,310 --> 00:28:37,770
大约消耗了它大约十多倍的能量
each bit costs it about 10 times more energy.

544
00:28:37,880 --> 00:28:41,380
对于苍蝇来讲，信息是非常昂贵的
Information is very expensive for a fly.

545
00:28:49,960 --> 00:28:53,720
一个能开很快的跑车
So, we have a sports car that has a very high top speed.

546
00:28:53,830 --> 00:28:55,880
拥有非常高的性能
It has a very high performance.

547
00:28:56,000 --> 00:28:59,150
同时，它也更耗油
But also, it has a very heavy fuel consumption.

548
00:28:59,270 --> 00:29:00,720
它需要大量的能量
It uses a lot of energy.

549
00:29:00,840 --> 00:29:03,060
我们在康桥闲逛
So, as we're idling along now in Cambridge,

550
00:29:03,170 --> 00:29:05,700
我们根本没有用到我们的高性能
we're not using any of our performance at all.

551
00:29:13,650 --> 00:29:15,960
这个小车性能略低
This little car here has a lot lower performance

552
00:29:16,080 --> 00:29:17,080
和跑车相比
than this sports car,

553
00:29:17,190 --> 00:29:18,610
但它更加经济
but it's much more economical.

554
00:29:18,720 --> 00:29:21,110
跑完相同的路程，它消耗更少的能量
It uses much less energy to go a given distance.

555
00:29:21,220 --> 00:29:23,510
所以你为高性能付出高价格
So, you pay a high price for high performance,

556
00:29:23,620 --> 00:29:24,910
神经元也一样
just like neurons.

557
00:29:25,030 --> 00:29:27,280
人类精力旺盛的大脑
The powerhouse brain of a human being

558
00:29:27,390 --> 00:29:31,120
比绿头苍蝇的大脑效率还要低
is even less fuel efficient than that of the blowfly.

559
00:29:31,230 --> 00:29:34,060
这就是之所以西蒙几乎肯定
And this is why Simon is almost certain

560
00:29:34,170 --> 00:29:37,790
人类的大脑已经达到了它的极限
that the human brain has reached its limit.

561
00:29:37,910 --> 00:29:40,730
如果绿头苍蝇就是这辆跑车
So, if the blowfly is this sports car,

562
00:29:40,840 --> 00:29:45,030
人类大脑，超高的性能
then the human brain, with it's vastly superior performance,

563
00:29:45,150 --> 00:29:47,270
就像火箭一样
is like a space rocket.

564
00:29:47,380 --> 00:29:51,410
大量的能量被用来处理信息
Huge amounts of energy being used to process information.

565
00:29:51,520 --> 00:29:55,140
人类大脑只占体重的2%
The human brain is only 2% of our body mass,

566
00:29:55,260 --> 00:30:00,080
但休息时，它占据了我们身体耗氧量的20%
but it consumes 20% of our oxygen when we are at rest.

567
00:30:00,190 --> 00:30:03,680
我们越聪明，就越耗能
The smarter we get, the higher the energy cost.

568
00:30:03,800 --> 00:30:06,720
如果我们想提升一成大脑性能
If we wanted to have a brain that was 10% better,

569
00:30:06,830 --> 00:30:09,860
大脑就必须增大两成
we might have to have one that was actually 20% bigger.

570
00:30:09,970 --> 00:30:14,240
它对身体的需求也会大增
Then it would make increasingly large demands on the body.

571
00:30:15,440 --> 00:30:16,900
如果人类大脑变得更大
If the human brain got bigger,

572
00:30:17,010 --> 00:30:20,770
小孩的出生会更困难
it would be more difficult to give birth to children.

573
00:30:20,880 --> 00:30:23,810
如果你想让它更大
And if you wanted to make it much bigger,

574
00:30:23,920 --> 00:30:25,440
当孩子出生时
when the child was born,

575
00:30:25,550 --> 00:30:28,640
大脑的发育就不会像现在这样好
the brain would have to be less well-developed than it is now.

576
00:30:28,760 --> 00:30:32,320
婴儿和幼年时期就会更长
So, infancy and childhood would last longer.

577
00:30:33,960 --> 00:30:37,720
进化已经事大脑达到某种平衡
Our brain has evolved to strike some sort of balance

578
00:30:37,830 --> 00:30:41,260
在处理信息的
between the cost of processing the information,

579
00:30:41,370 --> 00:30:42,790
非常高的消耗
which is very high,

580
00:30:42,900 --> 00:30:45,630
和我们实际需要处理的信息量之间
and the amount of information we actually need to process.

581
00:30:45,740 --> 00:30:50,730
但是仍然有使我们变得聪明的方法
But there may still be a way for us to become smarter.

582
00:30:50,840 --> 00:30:53,900
如果我们亲自接手
If we take matters into our own hands,

583
00:30:54,010 --> 00:30:58,370
我们可以创造一个超级种族
we may build a superior human race.

584
00:30:58,490 --> 00:31:02,120
只有我们当中的一部分 才会有幸成为
And only some of us will be part of it.

585
00:31:04,860 --> 00:31:06,680
所有的现代人类
All of modern humanity

586
00:31:06,790 --> 00:31:11,150
都可回溯到五万年前
can trace its ancestry back to a small group of people

587
00:31:11,260 --> 00:31:15,120
居住在东非的一小群人
living in East Africa about 50,000 years ago.

588
00:31:15,230 --> 00:31:18,360
当时我们看起来非常相似
We all looked very similar back then.

589
00:31:18,470 --> 00:31:19,790
一千年以来
Over the millennia,

590
00:31:19,910 --> 00:31:22,400
我们适应了当地的气候
we've adapted to our local climates

591
00:31:22,510 --> 00:31:26,100
成为当今各种不同的人
and become the rainbow of people we are today.

592
00:31:26,210 --> 00:31:28,940
但是进化没有停止
But evolution hasn't stopped.

593
00:31:29,050 --> 00:31:31,770
我们将朝向何方？
Where are we headed?

594
00:31:31,880 --> 00:31:34,710
将来的超级人类会不会……
Could a future race of superior humans...

595
00:31:34,820 --> 00:31:38,420
看起来就像这样？
Look like this?

596
00:31:41,390 --> 00:31:44,780
彼得·沃德 是古生物学家
Peter Ward is a paleontologist

597
00:31:44,900 --> 00:31:48,520
西雅图的华盛顿大学
at the University of Washington in Seattle.

598
00:31:48,630 --> 00:31:51,120
极尽他所见之处，都能看到进化
He finds evolution everywhere he looks,

599
00:31:51,240 --> 00:31:54,690
甚至在卖鱼的市场中
even inside a fish market.

600
00:31:54,810 --> 00:31:57,500
我们有西北地区两种标准的
Well, we got two really standard wonderful food fish

601
00:31:57,610 --> 00:31:58,970
美味的食用鱼
in the Northwest.

602
00:31:59,080 --> 00:32:02,200
大比目鱼和这些漂亮的鲑鱼
We've got this nice big halibut and these beautiful salmon.

603
00:32:02,310 --> 00:32:04,200
事实上，如果来看化石记录
If we look at the fossil record, actually,

604
00:32:04,320 --> 00:32:05,940
这些要原始得多
these are way more primitive.

605
00:32:06,050 --> 00:32:08,110
先有这些家伙，事实上
These guys were here first and, in fact,

606
00:32:08,220 --> 00:32:11,840
多得多的鱼长成这样，漂亮的梭形
far more fish look like this -- that beautiful fusiform shape --

607
00:32:11,960 --> 00:32:13,880
比起这些丑东西
than this rather ugly thing.

608
00:32:13,990 --> 00:32:16,380
就像被挤扁碾平了一样
It's been squished down and flattened.

609
00:32:16,500 --> 00:32:19,550
比如鲑鱼，它翻转过来
It's like taking a salmon, rolling it on its side,

610
00:32:19,670 --> 00:32:21,150
把眼睛转过来
bringing an eye over,

611
00:32:21,270 --> 00:32:24,820
永远以这种旋转过的形状生存
and living forever with that totally rotated shape.

612
00:32:24,940 --> 00:32:27,090
这家伙生活在水底
This guy lives on the bottom,

613
00:32:27,210 --> 00:32:30,660
这是对其生存地点极好的适应
and this is a superb adaptation for where it lives.

614
00:32:30,780 --> 00:32:35,170
从鲑鱼起，进化创造了比目鱼
From the salmon, evolution created the halibut.

615
00:32:35,280 --> 00:32:40,110
彼得所怀疑的是，这一切会发生在人类身上吗？
What, Peter wonders, might it do to humans?

616
00:32:40,220 --> 00:32:43,680
如果相信大自然计划使我们进化得
It is tempting to believe that nature has a master plan

617
00:32:43,790 --> 00:32:48,180
更健康、聪明、有吸引力，那会是很诱人的
to evolve us into fitter, smarter, more attractive beings.

618
00:32:48,290 --> 00:32:52,020
但自然并不这样工作
But nature doesn't work that way.

619
00:32:52,130 --> 00:32:55,960
我们最好的和最糟的特性
Our best traits and our worst traits

620
00:32:56,070 --> 00:32:59,530
被随机地选择给我们
are chosen for us quite randomly.

621
00:32:59,640 --> 00:33:01,890
阿尔伯特·爱因斯坦一句著名的话
Albert Einstein most famously said,

622
00:33:02,010 --> 00:33:04,700
“上帝不会和宇宙玩骰子”
"God does not play dice with the universe."

623
00:33:04,810 --> 00:33:06,800
在物理学中，这可能是对的
Well, maybe he was right in physics,

624
00:33:06,910 --> 00:33:09,740
但在进化中，到处都是骰子游戏
but in evolution, there's a whole lot of dice playing.

625
00:33:09,850 --> 00:33:12,940
这是我的进化骰子
Here's my evolutionary dice -- die.

626
00:33:13,050 --> 00:33:17,110
有A、T、G和C，基因编码
This is A, T, G, and C -- the genetic code.

627
00:33:17,220 --> 00:33:19,210
当他们结合在一起时
When they combine together,

628
00:33:19,330 --> 00:33:21,910
他们决定了有机体将会具有的特性
they tell an organism what traits it's gonna have.

629
00:33:22,030 --> 00:33:23,550
许多这种组合
And much of that combination

630
00:33:23,660 --> 00:33:25,650
是以随机方式实现的
comes together in random fashion.

631
00:33:25,760 --> 00:33:28,960
如果我投掷进化骰子
So if I throw this evolutionary die,

632
00:33:29,070 --> 00:33:32,960
我卡在这个特定的基因处
I'm gonna be stuck with this particular gene,

633
00:33:33,070 --> 00:33:36,960
这个基因会把我带到四条路中任意一条上去
and that gene might take me up any one of these four roads.

634
00:33:37,080 --> 00:33:39,730
其中的三条可能会立即杀死你
Three of those roads might kill you almost instantly.

635
00:33:39,850 --> 00:33:43,950
另一条可能会通向真正的优秀有机体
One of them might be towards a really superior organism.

636
00:33:46,180 --> 00:33:49,580
如果彼得想到达城镇另一边
If Peter wants to make his way across town

637
00:33:49,690 --> 00:33:51,280
去一个高档餐厅
to a high-end restaurant

638
00:33:51,390 --> 00:33:54,410
使用DNA导航法则
using the rules of DNA navigation,

639
00:33:54,530 --> 00:33:59,100
他会依靠骰子的随机滚动
he will have to rely on the random roll of a die.

640
00:34:02,270 --> 00:34:04,430
一次投掷，会让他更进一步
One turn could get him closer.

641
00:34:06,440 --> 00:34:09,260
下一次可能会让他返回出发点
The next could turn him back toward where he started.

642
00:34:09,380 --> 00:34:13,170
无法预言他何时或者能否到达
There's no telling when or if he will ever make it.

643
00:34:13,280 --> 00:34:15,770
进化是一个随机过程
Evolution is a random process

644
00:34:15,880 --> 00:34:19,010
而且通常会走投无路
that usually leads to genetic dead ends.

645
00:34:19,120 --> 00:34:20,420
呃！
Ugh!

646
00:34:21,590 --> 00:34:24,480
但是如果彼得能够离开
But what if Peter could escape the randomness

647
00:34:24,590 --> 00:34:26,480
随机的自然选择
of natural selection?

648
00:34:26,590 --> 00:34:29,350
直到我们成为技术物种
Up until we became a technical species,

649
00:34:29,460 --> 00:34:31,850
和其他物种一样，我们
we were, like every other species,

650
00:34:31,960 --> 00:34:33,590
依赖于随机选择的恩泽
at mercy to the randomness

651
00:34:33,700 --> 00:34:36,920
真的，进化真是恶心
and to, really, the nastiness of evolution.

652
00:34:37,040 --> 00:34:38,820
人类下一阶段的进化
The next stage of human evolution

653
00:34:38,940 --> 00:34:40,630
将成为我们人类自己
is going to be we humans

654
00:34:40,740 --> 00:34:44,680
来修补基因组本身
tinkering right into the genome itself.

655
00:34:46,080 --> 00:34:50,070
不但可以改变人生
Not only can we change people in their lifetime,

656
00:34:50,180 --> 00:34:54,010
我们还将改变特定的DNA
but we'll be able to change their very DNA

657
00:34:54,120 --> 00:34:55,810
他们，和他们的变化
so that they and their changes

658
00:34:55,920 --> 00:34:58,280
将会以传给下一代
get passed on to the next generation.

659
00:34:58,390 --> 00:35:01,150
通过这种定向的进化
With this type of directed evolution,

660
00:35:01,260 --> 00:35:04,420
彼得不必盲目依赖于
Peter does not have to blindly depend on chance

661
00:35:04,530 --> 00:35:07,660
通往想去地方的机遇
to get him where he wants to go.

662
00:35:10,070 --> 00:35:12,140
滨水海鲜烧烤
Waterfront Seafood Grill.

663
00:35:18,240 --> 00:35:19,970
基因技术
Genetic technology

664
00:35:20,080 --> 00:35:23,140
将你直接从A点送达B点
could take you on a direct route from "A" to "B",

665
00:35:23,250 --> 00:35:25,140
只要你能负得起路费
as long as you can pay for the ride.

666
00:35:25,250 --> 00:35:27,440
彼得相信，人类一旦
Peter believes that once humans

667
00:35:27,550 --> 00:35:29,980
开始追求人工选择
start pursuing unnatural selection,

668
00:35:30,090 --> 00:35:33,210
我们将被划分为两个种族——
we will diverge into two separate races --

669
00:35:33,330 --> 00:35:36,620
那些还在依赖进化骰子的
those who are left rolling the evolutionary dice

670
00:35:36,730 --> 00:35:41,320
和那些有能力负担设计自己基因组的
and those who can afford to design their own genome.

671
00:35:41,430 --> 00:35:44,120
好的，多谢
All right. Thank you much.

672
00:35:44,240 --> 00:35:46,430
大比目鱼，谢谢
Halibut. Thanks.

673
00:35:46,540 --> 00:35:49,430
我可以预见
I could see a point where once the differences

674
00:35:49,540 --> 00:35:51,100
一旦我们改造自己
we engineer into ourselves

675
00:35:51,210 --> 00:35:53,600
产生巨大的差别，
are so different from what you find

676
00:35:53,710 --> 00:35:55,600
那么相对那些原始平常的人类
in the wild, stock humans

677
00:35:55,710 --> 00:35:58,500
和基因工程改造的人类之间
versus the genetically engineered humans,

678
00:35:58,620 --> 00:36:00,340
将会产生分隔
there will be a divergence.

679
00:36:00,450 --> 00:36:02,580
如果你让
If you had children that bred

680
00:36:02,690 --> 00:36:04,210
那些功能增强的孩子们结婚生子
with other children with these enhancements,

681
00:36:04,320 --> 00:36:05,610
继续下去
it keeps going.

682
00:36:05,720 --> 00:36:08,550
将会看到社会的两极分化
You're gonna see a social drifting apart.

683
00:36:08,660 --> 00:36:10,380
当基因库分离之时
And speciation happens

684
00:36:10,500 --> 00:36:15,050
就是新物种形成之日
when gene pools separate -- populations separate.

685
00:36:15,170 --> 00:36:18,160
这是冷酷无情的未来
It is a grim vision of the future --

686
00:36:18,270 --> 00:36:20,360
全球封闭的社区
a global gated community

687
00:36:20,470 --> 00:36:24,500
居住高富帅白富美，长寿的人类2.0版
of super-rich, long-lived human 2.0's

688
00:36:24,610 --> 00:36:27,170
霸占所有的资源
hogging all the resources...

689
00:36:27,280 --> 00:36:30,870
而那些完全被分隔的人类物种
And a completely separate species of people

690
00:36:30,980 --> 00:36:33,110
只能勉强度日
who can barely survive.

691
00:36:33,220 --> 00:36:35,010
但是另外的技术
But another technological course

692
00:36:35,120 --> 00:36:38,220
可以带领我们通往不同方向
is driving us in a different direction.

693
00:36:39,760 --> 00:36:42,950
通往创造超级人类种族的下一个大飞跃
The next big leap toward creating a superior human race

694
00:36:43,060 --> 00:36:45,550
也可以是我们将差异放在一边
may come from us putting aside our differences

695
00:36:45,660 --> 00:36:49,200
手挽手向前
and putting our heads together.

696
00:36:51,450 --> 00:36:54,270
地球已然是拥挤的地方
Earth is already a crowded place.

697
00:36:54,380 --> 00:36:56,170
本世纪末
And by the end of this century,

698
00:36:56,290 --> 00:37:00,480
人口数量可能会到达110亿
the human population will probably reach 11 billion.

699
00:37:00,590 --> 00:37:04,610
我们将会像蜂巢中蜜蜂般拥挤
We'll be packed in as tightly as bees in a hive.

700
00:37:04,730 --> 00:37:07,180
这种预测激发一些科学家的灵感
That prospect has inspired some scientists

701
00:37:07,300 --> 00:37:10,150
创造人类新的进化
to envision a new evolution of mankind.

702
00:37:10,270 --> 00:37:13,260
就像昆虫群落，共享工作量
Just as insect colonies share the workload,

703
00:37:13,370 --> 00:37:17,390
也许我们还会拥有并行大脑
perhaps we can learn to harness the power of multiple brains

704
00:37:17,510 --> 00:37:22,440
创造非常先进的蜂巢般 全球集群分布式思维
and create a vastly superior global hive mind.

705
00:37:25,410 --> 00:37:28,370
仙迪·彭特兰是麻省理工学院人类动力实验室
"Sandy" Pentland is the head of the Human Dynamics Lab

706
00:37:28,480 --> 00:37:29,770
的领导人
at M.I.T.

707
00:37:29,890 --> 00:37:32,510
他是新研究领域的先锋
He's a pioneer of a new field of research --

708
00:37:32,620 --> 00:37:35,310
